    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_923aba45cc96cb05bff6b80240105515582d781e378eee3b4f56e5b75132aff7",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The computation turns approximate values of Z(t) into usable sign evidence by pairing each value with a conservative error allowance: the source gives safe bounds of 3×10^-5 and 2.1×10^-11 for methods A and B but says the program used the looser fixed bounds 10^-4 and 2.5×10^-7. Interpreted as an enclosure, a computed value whose error interval excludes zero has a controlled sign; the reported success of method B at every Gram point where it was needed supplies the observed sign-resolution fact. This representation gains robust discrete signs from continuous approximations but discards magnitude information and does not by itself certify that all zeros were found.",
