    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_ddfdfbfd35e93ca56eca36b01f8d303f61815691abeb25f1407c5157b03e0f66",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "A finite critical-line computation can be packaged as a parameterized interface for other theorems: the computational and analytic chain certifies that all relevant zeros below a larger height A lie on the critical line, and literature results conditional on such an A may then consume the improved bound. This makes bounded verification mathematically reusable while leaving it logically distinct from the unbounded Riemann hypothesis.",
