    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_f82e76e0b6ac1bed3639a00cdf8ddc7c918968ebd3260720162e191e8c6af0d3",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Rosser-rule exceptions connect an aggregate census audit to a local search-radius obligation. The interval-count relation is applicable only when no displaced missing pair crosses the chosen endpoints; operationally, every observed exception was repaired by finding that pair in an immediately preceding or succeeding block. The proposed 0 0 1 3 pattern and its reversal then expose why an adjacent-block-only repair is a heuristic rather than a complete search rule, while the exception taxonomy records which local displacement geometries have actually occurred.",
